[center][b][size=4]This is a framework, not a mod you play. If another mod told you to install it, turn it on and you're done. Everything below is for mod authors.[/size][/b]
[/center]
[line]

Two mods both want to drive the same follower. One walks him to a merchant, the other tells him to hold position, and they fight over him every frame. That is the problem Harbinger solves. It sits underneath the mods on your load order and decides who is allowed to control an NPC at any moment, so one mod's orders go through and the others stand down cleanly instead of clobbering each other.

Harbinger does not make an NPC do anything on its own. It moderates. A client mod brings the behaviour through the normal engine mechanisms it already uses, and Harbinger only decides whether that behaviour is allowed right now. It routes control from the mod that should have it and denies the sources that would compete, at the point the NPC receives them, so the winning behaviour just runs.

[size=5][b]For players[/b][/size]

You install it because something else needs it. There is nothing to configure and no ESP to sort. It edits no records and adds no scripts to your NPCs, and with no client mod asking for anything it does nothing at all. An NPC that no mod is currently steering behaves exactly as it would without Harbinger.

[size=5][b]For mod authors[/b][/size]

Harbinger is a C ABI you talk to from your own SKSE plugin. You claim a control facet on an actor, aggression, combat target, casting, a package, and so on, at a priority you choose. If another client claims the same facet, the higher priority owns it and the loser waits, and the facet is restored the moment the last claim is released. You keep driving the NPC with your own code. Harbinger's job is to make sure the engine and the other mods let you.

The design rule is that Harbinger owns [i]whether[/i] something happens, and your mod owns [i]what[/i] should happen. So it never generates behaviour and never fights the engine. It shapes what the NPC is allowed to receive, at the source, and the engine does the rest.

Some facets are proven in the field already, through [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/000000]marth's Follower Overhaul[/url], which is a full Harbinger client and the worked example. Others are built and ready but a mod author would be the first to run them in earnest. The page is honest about which is which. The full capability list, the proven-versus-untested status of each, and the header you build against are in the [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/APMF]GitHub repo[/url].

Coexistence is the whole point, so it is a hard rule and not a hope. Harbinger only ever hooks in ways that chain with other mods, it reads the engine's own answer before it changes anything, and it verifies what it is hooking at install. If it cannot do a thing safely it declines the thing, it does not crash your game.

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]SKSE64[/*]
[*]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/*]
[*]No DLC required.[/*]
[/list]

This needs Anniversary Edition (1.6.x). Skyrim SE 1.5.x and VR are not supported.

[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]

Install it with your mod manager like any other SKSE plugin. There is no plugin file to enable and load order does not matter. Launch through SKSE.

[size=5][b]FAQ[/b][/size]

[b]I was sent here by another mod. Do I need to set anything up?[/b]
No. Install it, turn it on, launch through SKSE. It has no MCM and nothing to tune.

[b]Will it conflict with my follower framework or AI overhaul?[/b]
No, that is what it is for. It edits no records and no AI packages, and it is built specifically to run alongside other mods that manage NPCs rather than fight them.

[b]Is it safe to add or remove mid-playthrough?[/b]
Yes. Its state lives in the SKSE co-save and reverts cleanly.

[b]I'm a mod author. Where do I start?[/b]
The header and the integration notes are in the [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/APMF]GitHub repo[/url]. You fetch one exported function after SKSE load, keep the pointer, and call it from your own plugin. It is a small surface.

[b]Does my client mod have to be written in C++?[/b]
Yes for now. Harbinger is a C ABI between SKSE plugins, so a client is a native plugin. A Papyrus-facing layer is not part of this release.

[size=5][b]A note on honesty[/b][/size]

This is deep engine work and it is new. The parts that are proven are proven because a shipping mod leans on them every session. The parts that are not, I have marked as not. If you build against it and something is wrong, tell me and I will fix it.

